+++
title: Nineveh
description: Ancient Assyrian city
hook: For about fifty years Nineveh was the largest city in the world. In 612
  BC, after a bitter period of civil war in Assyria, a coalition of its former
  subject peoples sacked it, and it was never a political or administrative
  centre again.
standfirst: "An ancient Near Eastern city of Upper Mesopotamia, on the eastern
  bank of the Tigris in the modern-day city of Mosul in northern Iraq: the
  capital and largest city of the Neo-Assyrian Empire, and potentially the
  wealthiest city in the ancient world."
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Mosul, Nineveh Governorate, Iraq
    from: "location: Mosul, Nineveh Governorate, Iraq"
  - name: Built
    value: c. 3000 BC
    from: "built: c. 3000 BC"
  - name: Capital of
    value: the Neo-Assyrian Empire — and potentially the wealthiest city in the
      ancient world
    from: It is located on the eastern bank of the [[ Tigris ]] River and was the
      capital and largest city of the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]], and
      potentially the wealthiest city in the ancient world.[[ ^1 ]]
  - name: Area of the site
    value: 7.5 km2 (2.9 sq mi)
    from: "area: 7.5 km2 (2.9 sq mi)"
  - name: Dug in
    value: 1845–1852, 1873–1874, 1885–1891, 1904–1905, 1929–1932, 1951–1958,
      1967–1971, 1987–1990, 2019–2023
    from: "excavation-dates: 1845–1852, 1873–1874, 1885–1891, 1904–1905,
      1929–1932, 1951–1958, 1967–1971, 1987–1990, 2019–2023"
  - name: Heritage status
    value: Tentative World Heritage Site, from 2000-07-07
    from: "heritage-designation: value: Tentative World Heritage Site from:
      2000-07-07"
pull-quotes:
  - text: Its inhabitants, young and old, I did not spare, and with their corpses
      I filled the streets of the city.
    attribution: Sennacherib, writing of Babylon
    section: sennacheribs-development-of-nineveh
  - text: And Hezekiah of Judah who had not submitted to my yoke ... him I shut up
      in Jerusalem his royal city like a caged bird. Earthworks I threw up
      against him, and anyone coming out of his city gate I made pay for his
      crime. His cities which I had plundered I had cut off from his land.
    attribution: Sennacherib, writing about a battle in Lachish
    section: sennacheribs-development-of-nineveh
  - text: turned into mounds and heaps
    attribution: The Babylonian Chronicles concerning the Fall of Nineveh — "but
      this is literary hyperbole"
    section: after-ashurbanipal
  - text: exceedingly great city of three days' journey in breadth
    attribution: The Book of Jonah (Jonah 3:3 and Jonah 4:11)
    section: biblical-nineveh
  - text: I did not learn that I was at so remarkable a spot, till near the river.
      Then they showed me a village on a great hill, which they call Nunia,
      and a mosque, in which the prophet Jonah was buried. Another hill in
      this district is called Kalla Nunia, or the Castle of Nineveh. On that
      lies a village Koindsjug.
    attribution: Carsten Niebuhr, who recorded the site's location during the
      1761–1767 Danish expedition
    section: archaeology
  - text: to obtain the largest possible number of well preserved objects of art
      at the least possible outlay of time and money
    attribution: Austen Henry Layard's stated goal
    section: archaeology
timeline:
  - when: 6000 BC
    what: Nineveh founded — as early as this date — during the late Neolithic
      period.
    from: Nineveh itself was founded as early as 6000 BC during the late [[
      Neolithic ]] period.
  - when: 5000 BC
    what: Nineveh transitions from a Halaf village to an Ubaid village.
    from: In 5000 BC, Nineveh transitioned from a [[ Halaf | halaf-culture ]]
      village to an [[ Ubaid | ubaid-period ]] village.
  - when: between 4500 and 4000 BC
    what: The settlement grows to 40 hectares in size.
    from: During the period between 4500 and 4000 BC it grew to 40 hectares in
      size.
  - when: 3000/2900–2600/2500 BC
    what: The Ninevite 5 period, defined primarily by the characteristic pottery
      found widely throughout Upper Mesopotamia.
    from: This period is known as //Ninevite 5//, or //Ninevite V//
      (3000/2900–2600/2500 BC) and is defined primarily by the characteristic
      pottery that is found widely throughout Upper Mesopotamia.[[ ^16 ]]
  - when: c. 1809–1775 BC
    what: Nineveh becomes part of the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia during the
      reign of Shamshi-Adad I, Amorite ruler of Ekallatum.
    from: In the early 2nd millennium BC Nineveh became part of the [[ Kingdom of
      Upper Mesopotamia ]] during the reign of [[ Shamshi-Adad I ]] (c.
      1809–1775 BC), Amorite ruler of [[ Ekallatum ]].[[ ^21 ]][[ ^22 ]]
  - when: 14th century BC
    what: The statue of the goddess is sent to Pharaoh Amenhotep III of Egypt, by
      orders of king Tushratta of Mitanni.
    from: The goddess's statue was sent to Pharaoh [[ Amenhotep III ]] of [[ Egypt
      ]] in the 14th century BC, by orders of the king [[ Tushratta ]] of [[
      Mitanni ]].
  - when: 1365 BC
    what: Ashur-uballit I reclaims the city while overthrowing the Mitanni Empire
      and creating the Middle Assyrian Empire (1365–1050 BC).
    from: The Assyrian king [[ Ashur-uballit I ]] reclaimed it in 1365 BC while
      overthrowing the Mitanni Empire and creating the [[ Middle Assyrian
      Empire ]] (1365–1050 BC).[[ ^26 ]]
  - when: c. 700 BC
    what: 'Sennacherib makes Nineveh a truly influential city: new streets and
      squares, and the South West Palace, the "palace without a rival".'
    from: It was [[ Sennacherib ]] who made Nineveh a truly influential city (c.
      700 BC), as he laid out new streets and squares, and built within it the
      South West Palace, or "palace without a rival", the plan of which has
      been mostly recovered and has overall dimensions of about 503 by 242
      metres (1,650 ft × 794 ft).
  - when: 701 BC
    what: The campaign leading up to the siege of Lachish, recorded in the set of
      reliefs called the "finest" from Sennacherib's reign.
    from: A full and characteristic [[ set shows the campaign leading up to the
      siege of Lachish | lachish-relief ]] in 701 BC; it is the "finest" from
      the reign of [[ Sennacherib ]], and now in the British Museum.[[ ^29 ]]
  - when: In around 627 BC
    what: Ashurbanipal dies and the Neo-Assyrian Empire begins to unravel through
      bitter civil wars between rival claimants for the throne.
    from: In around 627 BC, after the death of its last great king [[ Ashurbanipal
      ]], the Neo-Assyrian Empire began to unravel through a series of bitter
      [[ civil wars | civil-war ]] between rival claimants for the throne, and
      in 616 BC Assyria was attacked by its own former vassals, the [[
      Chaldeans | chaldea ]], [[ Babylonians | babylonia ]], [[ Medes ]], and
      [[ Scythians ]].
  - when: 616 BC
    what: Assyria is attacked by its own former vassals — the Chaldeans,
      Babylonians, Medes and Scythians — and Kalhu is sacked.
    from: In about 616 BC [[ Kalhu | nimrud ]] was sacked, the allied forces
      eventually reached Nineveh, besieging and sacking the city in 612 BC,
      following bitter house-to-house fighting, after which it was razed.
  - when: 612 BC
    what: Nineveh besieged and sacked after bitter house-to-house fighting, and
      then razed.
    from: In about 616 BC [[ Kalhu | nimrud ]] was sacked, the allied forces
      eventually reached Nineveh, besieging and sacking the city in 612 BC,
      following bitter house-to-house fighting, after which it was razed.
  - when: 605 BC
    what: The Assyrian Empire comes to an end, the Medes and Babylonians dividing
      its colonies between themselves.
    from: The Assyrian Empire then came to an end by 605 BC, the Medes and
      Babylonians dividing its colonies between themselves.
  - when: 539/538 BC
    what: The Cyrus Cylinder — possibly the earliest written evidence that Nineveh
      persisted as a settlement, though the reading is disputed.
    from: The earliest piece of written evidence for the persistence of Nineveh as
      a settlement is possibly the [[ Cyrus Cylinder ]] of 539/538 BC, but the
      reading of this is disputed.
  - when: AD 50
    what: According to Tacitus, Meherdates, a claimant to the Parthian throne with
      Roman support, takes Nineveh.
    from: According to [[ Tacitus ]], in AD 50 [[ Meherdates ]], a claimant to the
      Parthian throne with Roman support, took Nineveh.[[ ^38 ]]
  - when: "554"
    what: Nineveh is a bishopric of the Church of the East.
    from: By the 2nd century AD there were [[ Christians ]] present and by 554 it
      was a [[ bishopric | adiabene-east-syriac-ecclesiastical-province ]] of
      the [[ Church of the East ]].
  - when: "627"
    what: The Battle of Nineveh, between the Eastern Roman Empire and the
      Sasanians, is fought at the city.
    from: In 627, the city was the site of the [[ Battle of Nineveh |
      battle-of-nineveh-627 ]] between the [[ Eastern Roman Empire |
      byzantine-empire ]] and the Sasanians.
  - when: "641"
    what: Conquered by the Arabs, who build a mosque on the west bank and turn it
      into an administrative centre.
    from: In 641, it was [[ conquered by the Arabs | muslim-conquest-of-persia ]],
      who built a [[ mosque ]] on the west bank and turned it into an
      administrative centre.
  - when: "1170"
    what: Benjamin of Tudela visits the site; Petachiah of Regensburg soon after.
    from: "[[ Benjamin of Tudela ]] visited it in 1170; [[ Petachiah of Regensburg
      ]] soon after.[[ ^5 ]]"
  - when: "1849"
    what: Layard rediscovers, in the Kuyunjiq mound, the lost palace of
      Sennacherib with its 71 rooms and colossal bas-reliefs.
    from: In the Kuyunjiq mound, Layard rediscovered in 1849 the lost palace of
      Sennacherib with its 71 rooms and colossal [[ bas-reliefs | bas-relief
      ]].[[ ^52 ]]
  - when: 2000-07-07
    what: Nineveh becomes a Tentative World Heritage Site.
    from: "heritage-designation: value: Tentative World Heritage Site from:
      2000-07-07"
  - when: July 24, 2014
    what: The Islamic State destroys the shrine on Nabī Yūnus, as part of a
      campaign against sanctuaries it deemed "un-Islamic" but also to loot the
      site through tunneling.
    from: On July 24, 2014, the [[ Islamic State ]] destroyed the shrine as part
      of a [[ campaign to destroy |
      destruction-of-cultural-heritage-by-the-islamic-state ]] religious
      sanctuaries it deemed "un-Islamic",[[ ^78 ]] but also to loot that site
      through tunneling.
  - when: Around April 13, 2016
    what: IS demolishes the Adad Gate and the adjacent wall by flattening them
      with a bulldozer.
    from: Around April 13, 2016, [[ IS | islamic-state ]] demolished both the gate
      and the adjacent wall by flattening them with a bulldozer.[[ ^84 ]][[
      ^80 ]]
  - when: October 2023
    what: An archaeological park is inaugurated at the site.
    from: In October 2023 an archaeological park was inaugurated at the site.
  - when: June 2026
    what: A six-foot-tall marble stele is discovered next to one of the gates; the
      cuneiform is not fully translated, but is thought to detail
      Ashurbanipal's building projects.
    from: Later in June 2026, a six-foot-tall marble stele next to one of the
      gates was discovered by a team of archaeologists. Although the cuneiform
      wasn't fully translated, it is thought to be detailing the building
      projects of Ashurbanipal.[[ ^77 ]]
image-plan:
  - file: Nineveh_-_Mashki_Gate.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The Mashki Gate, reconstructed in fortified mudbrick to the height of
      the top of the vaulted passageway — and bulldozed along with the Nergal
      and Adad Gates during [[ IS | islamic-state ]] occupation.[[ ^80 ]]
  - file: View_of_the_village_of_Nunia_or_Ninive,_Niebuhr_1778.jpg
    role: inline
    section: geography
    caption: View of the village of "Nunia" or "Ninive", published by [[ Carsten
      Niebuhr ]] in 1778
  - file: Views_of_the_archaeological_site_of_Nineveh_in_modern-day_Mosul,_currently_occupied_by_squatters_06_(cropped).jpg
    role: inline
    section: geography
    caption: "A village standing in Nineveh in 2019: roughly a third of the walled
      site is overlaid by the Nebi Yunus suburbs of eastern [[ Mosul ]].[[ ^12
      ]]"
  - file: British_Museum_Middle_east_14022019_Beveled_rim_bowl_Nineveh_Late_Uruk_Period_3672.jpg
    role: inline
    section: uruk-period
    caption: Beveled rim bowl. Clay, from Nineveh, Late Uruk Period, 3300-3100 BC.
      British Museum. Bowls like it were among the finds from the city's
      period as a trade colony of [[ Uruk ]].[[ ^14 ]][[ ^15 ]]
  - file: Polychrome_painted_jar,_geometric_designs_and_animals,_the_so-called_"Scarlet_Ware"._From_Iraq,_Tell_Abu_Qasim_(Arabic_تل_ابو_قاسم),_Hamrin_Basin,_Diyala_Valley._2800-2000_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ninevite-5-period
    caption: Polychrome painted jar, geometric designs and animals, the so-called
      "Scarlet Ware". From Tell Abu Qasim at Hamrin Basin, Iraq. 2800–2600
      BCE. Iraq Museum. Iraqi [[ Scarlet Ware | diyala-river#scarlet-ware ]]
      culture also belongs to the Ninevite 5 period.
  - file: Relief_from_Nineveh_Vorderasiatisches_Museum_Berlin.jpg
    role: inline
    section: sennacheribs-development-of-nineveh
    caption: Refined low-relief section of a bull-hunt frieze from Nineveh, [[
      alabaster ]], c. 695 BC ([[ Pergamon Museum ]], Berlin)
  - file: Nineveh_north_palace_king_hunting_lion.jpg
    role: full
    section: sennacheribs-development-of-nineveh
    caption: Relief of [[ Ashurbanipal ]] hunting a [[ Mesopotamian lion ]],[[ ^27
      ]] from the Northern Palace in Nineveh, as seen at the [[ British Museum
      ]]
  - file: 2018_Ashurbanipal_-_Nineveh.jpg
    role: full
    section: after-ashurbanipal
    caption: The walls of Nineveh at the time of Ashurbanipal. 645–640 BC. [[
      British Museum ]] BM 124938.[[ ^33 ]]
  - file: Rembrandt_Harmenszoon_van_Rijn_-_The_Prophet_Jonah_before_the_Walls_of_Nineveh,_c._1655_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg
    role: inline
    section: biblical-nineveh
    caption: //The Prophet Jonah before the Walls of Nineveh//, drawing by [[
      Rembrandt ]], c. 1655. In the [[ Book of Jonah ]], God sent Jonah to
      preach to the Ninevites of their coming destruction, and they fasted and
      repented.
  - file: Nineve.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: Bronze lion from Nineveh — one of the large numbers of [[ Assyrian
      sculptures | assyrian-sculpture ]] and other artifacts excavated from
      the ruins and now located in museums around the world.
  - file: Humvee_down_after_isis_attack.jpg
    role: drop
    section: archaeological-remains
    caption: Humvee down after ISIL attack
  - file: Nineveh_Nebi_Yunus_Excavation_Bull-Man_Head.JPG
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-remains
    caption: '[[ Winged Bull | lamassu ]] excavated at Tell Nebi Yunus by Iraqi
      archaeologists; of the "bull-man" sculptures found in the monumental
      entryway there in 1990, some were apparently unfinished.'
  - file: Nineveh_map_city_walls_&_gates.JPG
    role: full
    section: archaeological-remains
    caption: Simplified plan of ancient Nineveh showing city wall and location of
      gateways. Six of the gateways have been explored to some extent by
      archaeologists.
  - file: Nineveh_Adad_gate_exterior_entrance_far2.JPG
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-remains
    caption: The restored [[ Adad ]] Gate, photographed before [[ IS |
      islamic-state ]] demolished both the gate and the adjacent wall by
      flattening them with a bulldozer around April 13, 2016.[[ ^84 ]][[ ^80
      ]]
  - file: Nineveh_walls_east_Shamash_Gate_from_rampart.JPG
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-remains
    caption: Eastern city wall and [[ Shamash ]] Gate, whose size and design
      suggest it was the most important gate in Neo-Assyrian times.
  - file: Fall_of_nineveh.jpg
    role: full
    section: in-popular-culture
    caption: "[[ John Martin | john-martin-painter ]], //The Fall of Nineveh// — a
      painting of the same name as, and inspired by, the epic of his friend [[
      Edwin Atherstone ]].[[ ^92 ]]"
glossary:
  - term: tell
    gloss: A mound-ruin; the two main ones inside the walls of Nineveh are Tell
      Kuyunjiq and Tell Nabī Yūnus.
    from: The two main [[ tells | tell-archaeology ]], or mound-ruins, within the
      walls are Tell Kuyunjiq and [[ Tell Nabī Yūnus | al-nabi-yunus-mosque
      ]], site of a shrine to [[ Jonah ]].
  - term: Nabī Yūnus
    gloss: The Arabic for "Prophet Jonah".
    from: //Nabī Yūnus// is the [[ Arabic ]] for "Prophet [[ Jonah ]]".
  - term: Kuyunjiq
    gloss: According to Layard a Turkish name, the diminutive of //koyun//
      ("sheep"); the Arabs also knew the mound as //Armousheeah//.
    from: //Kuyunjiq// was, according to [[ Layard | austen-henry-layard ]], a
      Turkish name; Layard used the form //kouyunjik//, the diminutive of
      //koyun// ("sheep") in Turkish. Kuyunjiq was also known as
      //Armousheeah// by the Arabs[[ ^9 ]]
  - term: Ninevite 5
    gloss: The period of 3000/2900–2600/2500 BC, defined primarily by the
      characteristic pottery that is found widely throughout Upper
      Mesopotamia.
    from: This period is known as //Ninevite 5//, or //Ninevite V//
      (3000/2900–2600/2500 BC) and is defined primarily by the characteristic
      pottery that is found widely throughout Upper Mesopotamia.[[ ^16 ]]
  - term: Early Jezirah
    gloss: A regional chronology developed by archaeologists for Upper
      Mesopotamia; in it, 'Ninevite 5' is equivalent to the Early Jezirah I–II
      period.
    from: Also, for the Upper Mesopotamian region, the //Early Jezirah//
      chronology has been developed by archaeologists. According to this
      regional chronology, 'Ninevite 5' is equivalent to the Early Jezirah
      I–II period.[[ ^17 ]]
  - term: lamassu
    gloss: "Colossal stone door figures weighing up to 30,000 kilograms (30 t):
      winged Mesopotamian lions or bulls, with human heads."
    from: Some of the principal doorways were flanked by colossal stone
      //lamassu// door figures weighing up to 30,000 kilograms (30 t); these
      were winged [[ Mesopotamian lions | asiatic-lion ]][[ ^27 ]] or [[ bulls
      | bull ]], with human heads.
  - term: Ba'uta d-Ninwe
    gloss: A fast observed by several Eastern churches, its name meaning
      //Nineveh's Prayer//.
    from: observe a fast called //Ba'uta d-Ninwe// (ܒܥܘܬܐ ܕܢܝܢܘܐ) which means
      //Nineveh's Prayer//.
  - term: Daesh
    gloss: ISIL, which occupied the area between 2014 and 2017.
    from: A major threat to Nineveh has been deliberate human actions by ISIL or
      Daesh, which occupied the area between 2014 and 2017.
further:
  - slug: neo-assyrian-empire
    why: Nineveh was its capital and largest city, and the architectural expansion
      from Ashurnasirpal II onward is the empire's own story.
  - slug: hanging-gardens-of-babylon
    why: Stephanie Dalley's argument that the garden Sennacherib built beside his
      palace at Nineveh, with its irrigation works, was the original.
  - slug: nimrud
    why: Kalhu, sacked in about 616 BC just before Nineveh, and one of the four
      corners of the quadrilateral that the "great city" of Genesis is
      generally regarded as consisting of.
anchors:
  hook: It was the [[ largest city | list-of-largest-cities-throughout-history
    ]] in the world for approximately fifty years until the year 612 BC when,
    after a bitter period of civil war in Assyria, it was [[ sacked |
    fall-of-nineveh ]] by a coalition of its former subject peoples including
    the [[ Babylonians ]], [[ Medes ]], and [[ Scythians ]]. The city was
    never again a political or administrative centre, but by [[ Late Antiquity
    ]] it was the seat of a bishop of the [[ Church of the East ]].
  standfirst: "!!Nineveh!![[ ^a ]] was an ancient Near Eastern city of [[ Upper
    Mesopotamia ]], located in the modern-day city of [[ Mosul ]] in northern
    [[ Iraq ]]. It is located on the eastern bank of the [[ Tigris ]] River
    and was the capital and largest city of the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]], and
    potentially the wealthiest city in the ancient world.[[ ^1 ]]"
