source:
  site: Wikipedia
  lang: en
  title: Nineveh
  url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nineveh
  page-id: 21699
  revision: "1365209712"
  modified: 2026-07-21T00:27:54Z
  retrieved: 2026-09-01
  license: CC BY-SA 4.0
  license-url: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
  attribution: This document contains text from the Wikipedia article "Nineveh"
    (revision 1365209712), by Wikipedia contributors, used under CC BY-SA 4.0.
rewritten:
  of: corpus/en/nineveh.mdy
  source-digest: 127d3e725b16d177
  source-revision: "1365209712"
  model: claude-opus-5
  left-out: "Five headings in the front matter — History, Chalcolithic, Early
    Bronze Age, Late Bronze and Iron Age — have no prose of their own in the
    source; they are present, in order, with empty bodies rather than with
    invented connective text. The Ninevite 5 section's five-image gallery
    (painted jar, painted bowl, Jemdet Nasr ware, Proto-Elamite ware,
    Kura-Araxtes jar) is not among the files listed in the front matter's
    image list, so it is left in the section body as it stood rather than
    moved into the image plan. The hero is the infobox photograph
    (Nineveh_-_Mashki_Gate.jpg), which comes from the front matter rather than
    that same image list. The infobox caption's claim that the Mashki Gate was
    \"since destroyed by the Islamic State\" is carried in the hero caption in
    the words of the article's own Mashki Gate entry, which cites it ([[ ^80
    ]]). The Humvee photograph is marked drop: the article says nothing about
    it beyond its caption. Two figure captions in the source that carried no
    citation (the Niebuhr view, the Scarlet Ware jar) still carry none,
    because the sentences they draw on are uncited in the article. One
    inconsistency is the document's own and is left standing rather than
    resolved: the infobox gives \"built: c. 3000 BC\" while the Neolithic
    section says Nineveh \"was founded as early as 6000 BC\"; the key fact
    quotes the infobox and the timeline quotes the prose."
  verified: false
  reviewed: false
+++
For approximately fifty years !!Nineveh!![[ ^a ]] was the [[ largest city | list-of-largest-cities-throughout-history ]] in the world. That ended in the year 612 BC, when, after a bitter period of civil war in Assyria, it was [[ sacked | fall-of-nineveh ]] by a coalition of its former subject peoples including the [[ Babylonians ]], [[ Medes ]], and [[ Scythians ]]. The city was never again a political or administrative centre.

It was an ancient Near Eastern city of [[ Upper Mesopotamia ]], on the eastern bank of the [[ Tigris ]] River, in what is now the city of [[ Mosul ]] in northern [[ Iraq ]]: the capital and largest city of the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]], and potentially the wealthiest city in the ancient world.[[ ^1 ]] Today the name is a common one for the half of Mosul that lies on the eastern bank of the Tigris, and the country's [[ Nineveh Governorate ]] takes its name from it.

By [[ Late Antiquity ]] the place was the seat of a bishop of the [[ Church of the East ]]. It declined relative to Mosul during the [[ Middle Ages ]] and was mostly abandoned by the 14th century AD after the massacres and dispersal of Assyrian Christians by [[ Timur ]].

Its ruins lie across the river from the historical city center of Mosul. The two main [[ tells | tell-archaeology ]], or mound-ruins, within the walls are Tell Kuyunjiq and [[ Tell Nabī Yūnus | al-nabi-yunus-mosque ]], site of a shrine to [[ Jonah ]], who according to the [[ Hebrew Bible ]] and the [[ Quran | jonah-in-islam ]] was a prophet who preached to Nineveh.[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]][[ ^4 ]] Large numbers of [[ Assyrian sculptures | assyrian-sculpture ]] and other artifacts have been excavated from the ruins of Nineveh, and are now located in museums around the world.

The location of Nineveh was known, to some, continuously through the Middle Ages. [[ Benjamin of Tudela ]] visited it in 1170; [[ Petachiah of Regensburg ]] soon after.[[ ^5 ]]

== Name

The English placename //Nineveh// comes from the [[ Latin ]] //Nīnevē// and the [[ Koine Greek ]] //Nineuḗ// (Νινευή) under influence of the [[ Biblical Hebrew ]] //Nīnəweh// (נִינְוֶה),[[ ^6 ]] from the [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] //Ninua// (var. //Ninâ//)[[ ^7 ]] or //Ninuwā//.[[ ^6 ]] The city was also known as //Ninuwa// in [[ Mari | mari-syria ]];[[ ^7 ]] //Ninawa// in [[ Aramaic | aramaic-language ]];[[ ^7 ]] Ninwe (ܢܝܢܘܐ) in [[ Syriac | syriac-language ]];[[ ^8 ]] and //Ninawa// (نینوا) in [[ Arabic ]].

The two mounds have names of their own. //Nabī Yūnus// is the [[ Arabic ]] for "Prophet [[ Jonah ]]". //Kuyunjiq// was, according to [[ Layard | austen-henry-layard ]], a Turkish name; Layard used the form //kouyunjik//, the diminutive of //koyun// ("sheep") in Turkish. Kuyunjiq was also known as //Armousheeah// by the Arabs,[[ ^9 ]] and it is thought to have some connection with the [[ Qara Qoyunlu ]] dynasty.[[ ^10 ]]

== Geography

The remains of ancient Nineveh — the areas of Kuyunjiq and Nabī Yūnus with their mounds — lie on a level part of the plain at the junction of the [[ Tigris ]] and the [[ Khosr Rivers | khosr-river ]], within an area of 750 hectares (1,900 acres)[[ ^11 ]] circumscribed by a 12-kilometre (7.5 mi) fortification wall. This whole extensive space is now one immense area of ruins, roughly a third of these overlaid by the Nebi Yunus suburbs of the city of eastern [[ Mosul ]].[[ ^12 ]]

The site is bisected by the [[ Khosr | khosr-river ]] river. North of the Khosr, the site is called //Kuyunjiq//, including the acropolis of Tell Kuyunjiq; the illegal village of Rahmaniye lay in eastern Kuyunjiq. South of the Khosr, the urbanized area is called //Nebi Yunus// (also Ghazliya, Jezayr, Jammasa), including Tell Nebi Yunus, where are the mosque of the Prophet Jonah, and a palace of [[ Esarhaddon ]]/[[ Ashurbanipal ]] below it. South of the street Al-'Asady — made by [[ Daesh | islamic-state ]] by destroying swaths of the city walls — the area is called //Junub Ninawah// or Shara Pepsi.

== History



== Neolithic

Nineveh itself was founded as early as 6000 BC during the late [[ Neolithic ]] period. [[ Deep sounding | vertical-electrical-sounding ]] at Nineveh uncovered soil layers that have been dated to early in the era of the [[ Hassuna | hassuna-culture ]] [[ archaeological culture ]].[[ ^13 ]] The development and culture of Nineveh paralleled [[ Tepe Gawra ]] and [[ Tell Arpachiyah ]] a few kilometers to the northeast. In the [[ Halaf Period | halaf-culture ]] it was a typical farming village.

== Chalcolithic



== Ubaid period

In 5000 BC, Nineveh transitioned from a [[ Halaf | halaf-culture ]] village to an [[ Ubaid | ubaid-period ]] village. During the Late Chalcolithic period it was one of the few Ubaid villages in Upper Mesopotamia which became a proto-city; others include [[ Ugarit ]], [[ Brak | tell-brak ]], [[ Hamoukar ]], [[ Arbela | erbil ]], [[ Alep | aleppo ]], and regionally [[ Susa ]], [[ Eridu ]], [[ Nippur ]]. During the period between 4500 and 4000 BC it grew to 40 hectares in size.

The greater Nineveh area is notable in the diffusion of metal technology across the near east as the first location outside of [[ Anatolia ]] to smelt copper. Tell Arpachiyah has the oldest copper smelting remains, and Tepe Gawra has the oldest metal work. The copper came from the mines at [[ Ergani ]].

== Uruk period

Nineveh IV became a trade colony of [[ Uruk ]] during the [[ Uruk Expansion ]] because of its location as the highest navigable point on the Tigris. It was contemporary with, and had a similar function to, [[ Habuba Kabira ]] on the Euphrates. Finds included [[ beveled rim bowls | beveled-rim-bowl ]].[[ ^14 ]][[ ^15 ]]

== Early Bronze Age



== Ninevite 5 period

Following the Late [[ Uruk period ]], the regional influence of Nineveh became particularly pronounced. This period is known as //Ninevite 5//, or //Ninevite V// (3000/2900–2600/2500 BC) and is defined primarily by the characteristic pottery that is found widely throughout Upper Mesopotamia.[[ ^16 ]] Also, for the Upper Mesopotamian region, the //Early Jezirah// chronology has been developed by archaeologists. According to this regional chronology, 'Ninevite 5' is equivalent to the Early Jezirah I–II period.[[ ^17 ]]

Ninevite 5 pottery is roughly contemporary to the [[ Early Transcaucasian culture ]] ware, and the [[ Jemdet Nasr period ]] ware.[[ ^16 ]] Iraqi [[ Scarlet Ware | diyala-river#scarlet-ware ]] culture also belongs to this period; this colourful painted pottery is somewhat similar to Jemdet Nasr ware. Scarlet Ware was first documented in the [[ Diyala River ]] basin in Iraq. Later, it was also found in the nearby [[ Hamrin Basin | lake-hamrin ]], and in [[ Luristan ]]. It is also contemporary with the [[ Proto-Elamite ]] period in Susa.

Ninevite 5 can be subdivided into the Early Ninevite 5 (3000-2750 BC) characterized by painted pottery and Late Ninevite 5 (2750-2500 BC) with incised pottery. In southern Mesopotamia, the former is contemporary with ED I-II, while the latter mirrors ED II-IIIA.[[ ^18 ]]

